24:\begin{frontmatter}
26:\title{Seeding metaheuristics with learned and heuristic solution pools for
27:the Interval Job Shop Scheduling Problem: an anytime study of when
28:initialisation helps}
30:\author[uniovi]{Hern\'an D\'iaz Rodr\'iguez}
31:\ead{herdiaz21@uniovi.es}
33:\affiliation[uniovi]{organization={Department of Computer Science, University of Oviedo},
34:            country={Spain}}
36:\begin{abstract}
37:Practitioners routinely assume that seeding a metaheuristic's initial
38:population with good solutions improves its results. We test it on the Job
39:Shop Scheduling Problem with interval processing times, using four
40:published solvers spanning the weak-to-strong axis, four seed generators
41:--- a deep reinforcement-learning policy and a ladder of randomised
42:constructives --- and a mixed pool, over 61 interval Taillard instances and
43:30 runs per cell (51{,}240 runs). A pilot study first identifies three
44:practices that manufacture positive seeding results: stagnation-triggered
45:stopping, single-budget comparison, and self-referential speed metrics.
47:With the instance as the unit of analysis, the sign of the effect is not
48:predicted by solver strength. A mixed pool improves the weakest solver by
49:1.14 percentage points of deviation from published lower bounds (95\% CI
50:$[-1.36,-0.93]$), leaves a memetic ABC unchanged, and does not harm a
51:genetic algorithm, although every single-generator pool does. On the
52:strongest solver the improvement is small, confined to one size class, and
53:absent once the analysis is restricted to instances whose control actually
54:converged. What matters more is the budget: at a tenth of ours, seeding
55:cuts the excess over the lower bound on all four solvers, but by margins
56:spanning an order of magnitude --- 11\% on the genetic algorithm against
57:40\% on the memetic ABC --- and the two largest gains accrue to the two
58:\emph{strongest} solvers, reversing the ordering at the budget limit.
60:Two observations constrain the mechanism. The mixed pool draws on the same
61:solutions as the single-generator arms and starts from a worse average
62:population, yet converges better, implicating composition rather than seed
63:quality. And premature convergence, the usual explanation for seeding
64:failures, is contradicted: the harmed genetic algorithm ends \emph{more}
65:dispersed than its control, not less.
66:\end{abstract}
68:\begin{keyword}
69:Job shop scheduling \sep interval uncertainty \sep population seeding \sep
70:metaheuristics \sep reinforcement learning \sep anytime behaviour
71:\end{keyword}
73:\end{frontmatter}
