76:\section{Introduction}\label{sec:intro}
78:Real-world scheduling rarely enjoys exact knowledge of task durations. When
79:only lower and upper bounds are available, the Job Shop Scheduling Problem
80:with interval processing times (IJSP) models each duration as a closed
81:interval and propagates interval arithmetic through the schedule
82:\cite{Lei2011,Lei2012}. The IJSP has accumulated a substantial algorithmic
83:literature over the last years, including genetic algorithms
84:\cite{DiazIPMU2020}, elitist artificial bee colony variants
85:\cite{DiazIWINAC2022,DiazESABC2023}, fast memetic ABC hybrids
86:\cite{DiazNatComp2023}, and, most recently, tabu-search hybrids built on
87:extreme-critical-path neighbourhoods that constitute the current state of
88:the art \cite{DiazTSN2}.
90:In parallel, learning-based methods have begun to produce fast constructive
91:policies for shop scheduling \cite{ZhangL2D2020,Bengio2021}. A deep
92:reinforcement-learning (RL) dispatching policy for the IJSP can emit
93:thousands of feasible, above-average solutions per instance in seconds,
94:raising an obvious question: should we seed the initial populations of
95:existing metaheuristics with such solutions? Folk wisdom answers yes, and
96:heuristic seeding of evolutionary algorithms has a long history
97:\cite{Kazimipour2014,Louis2004}. Yet the empirical evidence behind that
98:wisdom is weaker than it appears. Seeding studies typically compare final
99:objective values under a fixed stopping rule, and our pilot experiments
100:show that this practice is fragile in three specific ways.
102:First, \emph{stagnation-triggered stopping}: under the customary
103:``$N$ iterations without improvement'' rule, a well-seeded population that
104:the solver cannot immediately improve triggers the rule at the floor value,
105:so seeded runs are cut short while unseeded runs search for an order of
106:magnitude longer --- the comparison silently penalises the seeds. Second,
107:\emph{budget-dependent gains}: under a fixed budget chosen without a
108:convergence check, seeded arms of weak solvers appear to win; with a
109:five-fold larger budget the unseeded control keeps improving, overtakes the
110:stagnating seeded population, and the sign of the effect \emph{reverses}.
111:Third, \emph{self-referential speed}: convergence speed is reported as the
112:time to the arm's own plateau, so an arm that stagnates early on a worse
113:value is credited with an acceleration. Any of these three artefacts can
114:manufacture a positive seeding result where none exists.
116:This paper makes three contributions.
117:\begin{enumerate}
118:\item \textbf{A pre-specified anytime methodology} for evaluating
119:  population seeding: convergence-checked final quality; two complementary
120:  time-to-target metrics (time to the arm's own plateau, and time to the
121:  unseeded control's final quality) reported with the proportion of
122:  instances on which the target is reached; and paired runs with per-cell
123:  budgets calibrated on the control.
124:\item \textbf{A portfolio study across the weak-to-strong axis} --- GA
125:  \cite{DiazIPMU2020}, elitist ABC \cite{DiazIWINAC2022}, memetic fEABC-LS
126:  \cite{DiazNatComp2023}, and the tabu hybrid TS-N2 \cite{DiazTSN2} ---
127:  showing that the sign of the seeding effect is \emph{not} predicted by
128:  solver strength: the weakest solver gains, the strongest gains on the
129:  largest instances, one is unaffected, and one is degraded. The sign
130:  tracks whether the solver's variation operators can consolidate a
131:  structurally heterogeneous population, and the magnitude tracks the
132:  ratio of instance size to search budget; both are associations our
133:  design supports rather than causal effects it isolates.
134:\item \textbf{Learned versus heuristic seeds}: the RL policy is compared,
135:  under identical injection protocols, against a quality ladder of
136:  randomised constructives (machine-operations-remaining, randomised
137:  Giffler--Thompson, and a tuned genetic-programming rule), plus a mixed
138:  pool assembled from the same solutions. The mixed pool starts from a
139:  \emph{worse} average population than its single-generator counterparts
140:  and still converges better, which points to pool composition rather than
141:  seed quality as the operative variable.
142:\end{enumerate}
144:Our results do not support the assumption we set out to test. A mixed seed
145:pool improves the weakest solver decisively, leaves a memetic ABC
146:unchanged, does not harm a genetic algorithm although every
147:single-generator pool does, and helps a strong tabu hybrid only where its
148:budget is tightest relative to instance size. The sign of the effect is
149:therefore not predicted by solver quality; what it tracks is whether the
150:solver's variation operators can exploit a structurally heterogeneous
151:population, and its magnitude tracks the ratio of instance size to search
152:budget. Section~\ref{sec:results} gives the figures.
154:The paper is organised as follows. Section~\ref{sec:related} reviews
155:related work. Section~\ref{sec:problem} defines the IJSP and the solver
156:portfolio. Section~\ref{sec:seeding} describes the seed generators, the
157:pool audit and the injection mechanism. Section~\ref{sec:pilot} reports the
158:pilot study whose failure modes motivate the design of
159:Section~\ref{sec:design}. Section~\ref{sec:results} presents the results,
160:Section~\ref{sec:discussion} discusses their implications, and
161:Section~\ref{sec:conclusions} concludes.
163:%% =========================================================================
164:\section{Related work}\label{sec:related}
166:\paragraph{Metaheuristics for the IJSP.} Lei introduced population-based
167:neighbourhood search for interval makespan minimisation \cite{Lei2011} and
168:a GA for interval due-date objectives \cite{Lei2012}. A GA tailored to
169:interval uncertainty with an insertion schedule-generation scheme was
170:proposed in \cite{DiazIPMU2020}, later outperformed by elitist ABC variants
171:\cite{DiazIWINAC2022}, a seasonal elitist ABC \cite{DiazESABC2023}, and the
172:fast elitist ABC with hill-climbing local search (fEABC-LS)
173:\cite{DiazNatComp2023}. The most recent entry couples the fEABC
174:framework with a best-improvement tabu search over block-boundary
175:neighbourhoods defined on both extreme scenario graphs \cite{DiazTSN2}. Our
176:portfolio comprises exactly these published solvers, replicated from their
177:original configurations (Section~\ref{sec:problem}).
179:\paragraph{Population initialisation and seeding.} Initialisation
180:strategies for evolutionary algorithms have been surveyed in
181:\cite{Kazimipour2014}; injecting known solutions has been studied as
182:case-injected genetic search \cite{Louis2004}, and constructive-heuristic
183:seeding is standard practice in scheduling applications. What distinguishes
184:the present study is not the mechanism --- ours is deliberately the
185:simplest possible: replace $k$ random individuals by pool solutions at
186:initialisation --- but the evaluation methodology: convergence checks,
187:anytime metrics, and paired statistics with multiple-comparison control.
189:%% TODO: 2-3 additional recent refs on RL-seeded metaheuristics if available.
191:\paragraph{Learning to construct schedules.} Deep RL dispatchers for the
192:deterministic JSP \cite{ZhangL2D2020} and the broader learn-to-optimise
193:programme \cite{Bengio2021} produce fast constructive policies whose
194:natural integration point with mature metaheuristics is precisely
195:initialisation. We treat the learned policy as one seed generator among
196:several, which allows the question \emph{is a learned constructive better
197:than a tuned heuristic one as a seed source?} to be answered under
198:identical injection protocols.
200:%% =========================================================================
201:\section{The IJSP and the solver portfolio}\label{sec:problem}
203:\subsection{Problem definition}
204:An IJSP instance consists of $n$ jobs, each a fixed sequence of $m$
205:operations, one per machine. The processing time of operation $o$ is a
206:closed interval $p_o=[p_o^-,p_o^+]$. Interval addition and component-wise
207:maximum extend the classical recursion for starting and completion times:
208:for a feasible processing order $\sigma$, the starting time of $o$ is
209:$s_o=\max(c_{PJ(o)},c_{PM(o)})$ with $c_o=s_o+p_o$, where $PJ$ and $PM$
210:denote job and machine predecessors and the maximum is taken
211:component-wise \cite{Lei2011,DiazIPMU2020}. The makespan
212:$C_{\max}=[C_{\max}^-,C_{\max}^+]$ is the component-wise maximum of job
213:completion times; it is exact, because the makespan is monotone in the
214:durations, so the all-lower and all-upper configurations realise its
215:bounds. Comparing schedules requires an interval ranking operator; we use
216:the expected-value ranking of \cite{Bustince2013}, i.e.\ the interval
217:midpoint, which we write $\mathbb{E}[C_{\max}]$ following the convention of
218:this literature; no probability distribution over the interval is assumed,
219:and the quantity is a ranking criterion rather than a mathematical
220:expectation. It ranks solutions for the evolutionary
221:solvers, following their original papers, while LEX2
222:(lexicographic-by-upper-bound) is used inside TS-N2, following \cite{DiazTSN2}; all
223:cross-arm comparisons are made on $\mathbb{E}[C_{\max}]$ within a single
224:solver, so the ranking choice never confounds the seeding effect.
226:Instances are the standard interval Taillard benchmark: each crisp duration
227:$p_o$ becomes $[p_o-\delta,\,p_o+\delta]$ with $\delta$ drawn uniformly
228:from $[0,\,0.15\,p_o]$ \cite{DiazIPMU2020,taillard1993benchmarks}.
230:\subsection{The solver portfolio}
231:All four solvers share the permutation-with-repetition encoding
232:\cite{Bierwirth1995}, the insertion schedule-generation scheme (active
233:schedules) for decoding, population size 250, and the component-wise
234:interval evaluation described above. Table~\ref{tab:portfolio} summarises
235:their published configurations, whose parameter settings we replicate
236:verbatim.
