296:\section{Seed pools and injection mechanism}\label{sec:seeding}
298:\subsection{Generators and pools}
299:For every instance we use pools of 1{,}024 i.i.d.\ solutions per generator,
300:each stored as a job-permutation plus its makespan interval under a
301:semi-active decoder. Four generators span a quality ladder:
302:\begin{itemize}
303:\item \textbf{MOR-$\varepsilon$}: most-operations-remaining dispatching
304:  with $\varepsilon=0.1$ random tie-perturbation (weak baseline);
305:\item \textbf{GT-$\varepsilon$}: randomised Giffler--Thompson with
306:  $\varepsilon$-noise inside the conflict set (active schedules; strong
307:  baseline);
308:\item \textbf{GP-$\varepsilon$}: a genetic-programming priority rule,
309:  evolved and irace-tuned offline, with GRASP-style $\varepsilon$-noise
310:  (strongest heuristic baseline);
311:\item \textbf{v2}: a deep-RL dispatching policy (deep-sets architecture,
312:  three independent training checkpoints), trained only on Taillard
313:  instances TA11--TA14 and developed/tuned on TA15--TA20.
314:\end{itemize}
315:Across the 71-instance pool collection the ladder is clean: mean relative
316:errors of 51.1\% (MOR) $>$ 30.3\% (GT) $>$ 21.4\% (GP) $\approx$ 21.2\%
317:(v2), with v2 exhibiting the highest structural diversity of the four
318:(0.95 vs.\ 0.92 for GP on a normalised disjunctive-order distance) and GP
319:slightly stronger on the largest size classes. A fifth, mixed pool
320:(equal parts v2/GT/GP; MOR is deliberately excluded, being the weakest
321:generator) is used by the \emph{mix} arm. All five pools --- the four generators and the mixture --- contain 1{,}024
322:solutions per instance, so no arm is sampled more richly than another.
324:The mixed pool interleaves the three generators one entry at a time, so
325:that any contiguous window of 250 entries contains $84/83/83$ seeds of
326:v2/GT/GP to within one seed, whatever its offset.
328:\subsection{Evaluation convention of the pools}\label{sec:audit}
329:Each pool line stores a permutation together with its interval makespan,
330:evaluated component-wise so that stored and solver-side objective values
331:coincide. The distinction matters because both interval conventions of
332:Section~\ref{sec:problem} occur in this literature and they do not agree:
333:against LEX2 aggregation the upper bound is identical on all
334:1{,}024 lines of every pool, while the lower bound differs on 1--22\% of
335:lines, by up to 98 time units on the $50\times20$ instances. Anyone
336:exchanging solutions between components that rank intervals differently
337:should expect the same.
339:\subsection{Injection mechanism}
340:Seeding is deliberately minimal. A seeded arm with fraction $k/250$
341:replaces the first $k$ individuals of the initial population by pool lines;
342:the remaining $250-k$ individuals are generated by the solver's standard
343:random initialisation. For paired run $r\in\{0,\dots,29\}$ the arm takes
344:pool lines $[(r\cdot k)\bmod 1024,\,(r\cdot k+k)\bmod 1024)$ --- consecutive
345:blocks, no reordering or filtering, so each run draws from the generator's
346:true output distribution rather than from a curated subset. The 30 blocks
347:start at 30 distinct offsets and consecutive ones are disjoint, but with
348:$k=250$ and 1{,}024 pool lines the wraparound makes any two of them share
349:$22\%$ of their entries on average, and each pool line is drawn by $7.3$
350:of the 30 runs; the seeded populations are therefore not fully independent
351:across runs, which is one reason the unit of analysis throughout is the
352:instance rather than the run. Scout-bee replacements
353:during the run remain fully random. Seeds enter as raw permutations and are
354:re-decoded and re-evaluated by the receiving solver, so no foreign
355:objective values ever enter the search.
