422:\section{Final experimental design}\label{sec:design}
424:\subsection{Instances}
425:All 60 interval Taillard instances TA1--TA70 excluding TA11--TA20, plus
426:ft10 (61 total). TA11--TA14 are the RL training set and TA15--TA20 the
427:RL/GP development set; excluding them is mandatory because they contaminate
428:the \emph{seed generators}. Twenty instances used for irace tuning of
429:TS-N2's hyper-parameters are retained but flagged: solver-tuning
430:contamination applies equally to every arm of that solver and cancels in
431:within-solver paired comparisons; a sensitivity analysis replicates all
432:conclusions with the flagged instances removed. Size classes range from
433:$15{\times}15$ to $50{\times}20$ (225--1{,}000 operations). Taillard's
434:largest class, TA71--TA80 ($100{\times}20$, 2{,}000 operations), is also
435:excluded: at twice the operation count of our largest class it falls
436:outside the budget calibration of Section~\ref{sec:design}, and extending
437:the design to it would require a separate calibration that we did not
438:perform. Consequently no seed pools were generated for either excluded
439:group.
441:\subsection{Arms}
442:Seven arms per solver: the unseeded control A0; the generator ladder
443:MOR, GT, GP, v2 and mix at the pre-specified fraction $p^{*}$; and a
444:v2@50\% contrast arm. The fraction sub-study (Phase~0, pre-specified
445:decision rule combining converged quality in ABCE3 and time-to-target in
446:TS-N2 over eight stratified instances) selected $p^{*}=100\%$: the benefit
447:of v2 seeding is monotone in the fraction on both criteria, with no
448:interior optimum.
449:%% TODO: FTOP sub-study outcome (elitist selection of the best 250 pool
450:%% lines vs iid sampling) — decides whether the ladder seeds iid or elite.
452:\subsection{Budgets and stopping}
453:Budgets are set per \emph{(solver, size class)} rather than per instance.
454:For each class we take one or two calibration instances, run the unseeded
455:control on them, and record $t_{\mathrm{conv}}$: the first CPU time at
456:which the run-averaged trace reaches within $0.1\%$ of its own final
457:value. The budget applied to every cell of that class is
458:\begin{equation}\label{eq:budget}
459:B(\text{solver},\text{class})=
460:\max\bigl\{60\,\mathrm{s},\;
461:\min\{1.5\,\tilde{t}_{\mathrm{conv}},\,900\,\mathrm{s}\}\bigr\},
462:\end{equation}
463:where $\tilde{t}_{\mathrm{conv}}$ is the median over that class's
464:calibration instances. The floor of 60\,s prevents degenerate budgets on
465:the small classes, where several solvers plateau in a few seconds. Every
466:arm of a cell receives the identical budget, which is what the
467:within-solver comparisons require. The eight calibration instances ---
468:\texttt{tai15\_15\_01}, \texttt{tai20\_20\_02}, \texttt{tai30\_15\_01},
469:\texttt{tai30\_20\_04}, \texttt{tai50\_15\_01}, \texttt{tai50\_15\_05},
470:\texttt{tai50\_20\_01} and \texttt{tai50\_20\_05} --- are stratified across
471:the size classes and are themselves members of the 61-instance evaluation
472:set; the control traces behind $t_{\mathrm{conv}}$ comprise five runs for
473:the GA and fEABC-LS and thirty for ABCE3 and TS-N2. The horizon of a class
474:is therefore the control's own convergence timescale, measured on one or
475:two of the instances to which it is later applied. What the endpoint
476:measures is thus performance at a control-calibrated horizon rather than a
477:solver-neutral approximation to convergence, a distinction we return to in
478:Section~\ref{sec:limits}.
480:The cap of 900\,s is a feasibility constraint rather than a methodological
481:choice. The design comprises 51{,}240 runs; at the budgets of
482:(\ref{eq:budget}) it consumed roughly 4{,}900 CPU-hours and fourteen days
483:of wall-clock time on fourteen concurrent workers. Raising the cap would
484:scale that figure proportionally on the classes that reach it, which are
485:also the slowest. We therefore accept the cap and treat its consequence
486:explicitly: on a substantial fraction of cells the unseeded control is
487:still improving when the budget expires, so those cells cannot support a
488:converged-quality claim. Section~\ref{sec:res-conv} identifies them and
489:reports the final-quality results both over all instances and restricted
490:to the cells that do satisfy the plateau rule.
492:\subsection{Statistics}
493:30 runs per cell. Run $r$ of every arm of a solver is launched with the
494:same RNG seed index, which blocks the design by (instance, run index).
495:This is \emph{not} a common-random-numbers coupling: a seeded arm does not
496:draw from the generator to build its first $k$ individuals, so the
497:generator state diverges between arms immediately after initialisation and
498:the runs follow unrelated random trajectories thereafter. The pairing is
499:therefore a labelling of blocks rather than a variance-reduction device.
500:Signed-rank tests on such pairs remain valid --- under the null the
501:per-instance differences are still symmetric about zero --- but they are
502:conservative rather than more powerful, and we make no claim of matched
503:trajectories. Within-solver comparisons use
504:paired Wilcoxon signed-rank tests with Holm correction per family, plus
505:Vargha--Delaney $\hat{A}_{12}$ effect sizes \cite{Vargha2000,Derrac2011};
506:generator rankings use Friedman tests with post-hoc analysis. Anytime
507:results are reported as expected-makespan-versus-CPU-time curves and as the
508:two time-to-target metrics of Section~\ref{sec:pilot}, the latter
509:accompanied by the proportion of instances on which the target is reached
510:--- which carries the information a success-rate curve would convey at the
511:budget limit.
513:%% =========================================================================
514:\section{Results}\label{sec:results}
516:All results below come from the full factorial design of
517:Section~\ref{sec:design}: 61 instances $\times$ 7 arms $\times$ 4 solvers
518:$\times$ 30 RNG-paired runs (51{,}240 runs). Every instance--arm cell
519:contains exactly 30 runs; no cell was imputed or dropped.
521:\subsection{Reference for relative deviations}\label{sec:rpd-ref}
523:Relative percentage deviation (RPD) is measured against published lower
524:bounds rather than against best-known values produced by our own group,
525:which would bias RPD downwards. Our interval instances are obtained from
526:Taillard's benchmark \cite{taillard1993benchmarks} by a symmetric
527:perturbation of each duration; we verified across all 57{,}450 operations
528:that $\mathrm{mid}(p_{ij})=c_{ij}$ exactly and that routings are unchanged.
529:Because the makespan is composed only of additions and maxima, and because
530:$\mathrm{mid}(A{+}B)=\mathrm{mid}(A)+\mathrm{mid}(B)$ while
531:$\mathrm{mid}(\max(A,B))\ge\max(\mathrm{mid}(A),\mathrm{mid}(B))$ under the
532:componentwise maximum, induction over the schedule DAG yields
533:$\mathrm{mid}(C_{\max}^{\mathrm{int}}(x))\ge C_{\max}^{\mathrm{crisp}}(x)$
534:for \emph{every} schedule $x$, and therefore
535:\begin{equation}\label{eq:lb}
536:\mathbb{E}[C_{\max}]^{*}\;\ge\;C_{\max}^{\mathrm{crisp}*}\;\ge\;\mathrm{LB},
537:\end{equation}
538:so the crisp lower bound is a valid (if not tight) bound for the interval
539:problem. We take LB from the consolidated table of
540:Coupvent des Graviers et al.\ \cite{coupvent2025bounds}; the instance
541:mapping was checked rather than assumed, with 19 of 60 instances showing
542:exact agreement between the published bound and the machine-load bound
543:recomputed from the instance files. For the remaining 41 the check is an
544:inequality, which is necessary but does not identify an instance, and we
545:do not claim more than that.
547:An exact identification is nevertheless available to any reader, and we
548:supply what it needs. Because each interval was built as
549:$[p_o-\delta,\,p_o+\delta]$, it is symmetric about the original crisp
550:duration, so the midpoint recovers that duration exactly; across the 61
551:instances every midpoint is integral, with no exception. We therefore
552:release the recovered crisp matrices, one per instance with a hash, so that
553:identification reduces to an element-wise comparison against Taillard's
554:published files. The recovery procedure itself is validated on the one
555:instance of the set whose crisp form is unambiguous: \texttt{ft10} recovers
556:to the Fisher--Thompson $10\times10$ matrix exactly.
558:Inequality~(\ref{eq:lb}) also yields a falsifiable prediction: no run may
559:ever fall below its bound. Across all 51{,}240 runs there is not a single
560:violation. This is a consistency check rather than a proof of correctness:
561:many errors would preserve the inequality, and it is the released crisp
562:matrices, not this check, that would settle the mapping. Moreover the bound
563:is
564:\emph{attained exactly} on six instances --- \texttt{tai30\_15\_05},
565:\texttt{tai50\_15\_01}, \texttt{tai50\_15\_02}, \texttt{tai50\_15\_04},
566:\texttt{tai50\_15\_07} and \texttt{tai50\_20\_09} --- where our portfolio
567:reaches $\mathbb{E}[C_{\max}]$ equal to the proven crisp optimum. By
568:(\ref{eq:lb}) these schedules are therefore optimal for the interval
569:instance as well, which both certifies optimality on those six instances
570:and confirms that the inequality is tight rather than vacuous.
572:\subsection{Which cells converged}\label{sec:res-conv}
574:The plateau rule of Section~\ref{sec:design} --- improvement in
575:$\mathbb{E}[C_{\max}]$ below $0.1\%$ over the final tenth of the budget ---
576:is applied to each control \emph{run} against the solution the solver
577:returns, rather than to the run-averaged curve, since averaging thirty
578:traces can hide a subset that is still improving; a cell counts as
579:converged when at least $90\%$ of its runs satisfy it. This classifies 111
580:of the 244 control cells as \emph{anytime-only}: 49 of 61 for the GA, 25
581:for fEABCLS, 22 for TSN2 and 15 for ABCE3. The GA fails on every instance
582:of the four largest classes, and the $50\times20$ class defeats every
583:solver but ABCE3, whose own failures are otherwise confined to
584:$15\times15$, where a third of its runs are still improving when the
585:$60$\,s floor stops them. What the classification is and is not sensitive
586:to is reported in Section~\ref{sec:res-threats}; the per-cell
587:classifications are released with the data.
