739:\paragraph{ABCE3: large and uniform gains.} The elitist bee colony is the
740:weakest solver in the portfolio (10.51\% RPD unseeded) and benefits
741:massively. MIX and V2 each win on 35 of 61 instances and V2H on 32, and all
742:three \emph{lose on none}, with $\hat{A}_{12}$ between 0.266 and 0.298.
743:Even GP, a mid-ranked generator elsewhere, wins 30 and loses none. This is
744:the textbook case: the solver's own diversification never reaches the
745:quality already present in the seeds.
747:\paragraph{TSN2: a gain confined to the largest instances.} The
748:tabu-search hybrid is the strongest solver, and the natural expectation ---
749:which our own pilot supported --- is saturation. It does not saturate, but
750:the effect must be stated carefully. MIX wins 15 instances and loses 5
751:($\Delta=-5.5$, $\hat{A}_{12}=0.436$), lowering RPD from 3.60\% to 3.41\%,
752:i.e.\ removing 5.3\% of the excess over the lower bound. It is also the
753:only arm that improves this solver: every other pool is neutral or harmful.
754:In aggregate the gain is small, and it is \emph{not} uniform: MIX is better
755:on 35 instances and worse on 22, with a median relative change of
756:$-0.03\%$. The gain is almost entirely attributable to one size class, and
757:we quantify this in Section~\ref{sec:res-size}. The defensible claim is
758:therefore not that seeding improves the strongest solver in general, but that it does
759:so on large instances under a search budget that is tight relative to
760:instance size.
762:\paragraph{fEABCLS: neutrality.} The memetic ABC is statistically
763:indistinguishable from its control under the three best generators (MIX
764:12/38/11, V2 7/44/10, V2H 7/43/11, all $\hat{A}_{12}\approx0.50$). Its
765:hill-climbing component reaches seed-level quality unaided within the
766:budget. Note that neutrality here is a property of the \emph{good} pools
767:only: MOR wins nothing and loses 56 of 61.
769:\paragraph{GA: harm, but only under an insufficient budget.} Over all 61
770:instances no arm improves the genetic algorithm: the best, MIX, is 13/29/19
771:with $\hat{A}_{12}=0.527$, and V2 is a clear loss (9/25/27). The
772:qualification of Section~\ref{sec:res-conv} is essential here. The GA fails
773:the plateau rule on 49 of 61 instances --- more than any other solver ---
774:and on the 12 where its control does converge the ordering reverses, with
775:MIX at 4.00\% against the control's 4.75\%. The reversal in the pooled
776:table is therefore a statement about the budget this solver needs, not
777:about seeding being harmful to it in principle.
779:Across all four solvers the generator ranking is stable in its extremes:
780:MOR is the worst seeded arm and GT the second worst on every solver (MOR
781:reaches $\hat{A}_{12}=0.920$ on the GA and $0.885$ on fEABCLS), while MIX
782:holds the best Friedman rank among seeded arms on all four
783:(Section~\ref{sec:res-ranking}). On mean RPD, MIX is the best seeded arm on
784:three solvers and is within 0.01 points of the best on fEABCLS. The
785:positions in between are not stable: GP outranks V2 on the GA but not on
786:the other three. The learned policy is thus competitive with the best tuned

 exited 1 in 569ms:
373:improve the injected solutions), while the unseeded control ran for
374:100--150 generations. The resulting ``final'' comparison charges the seeds
375:with the solver's inability to improve them. Any seeding study that uses
376:stagnation-based stopping inherits this bias.
378:\subsection{Artefact 2: budget-dependent gains that reverse}
379:Re-running everything under a fixed budget of 200 generations (no
380:stagnation rule) appeared to settle matters: seeded GA arms beat the
381:control on the hardest instance by 34 units of expected makespan. A
382:convergence check, however, showed the GA control still improving at
383:budget exhaustion. At $5\times$ the budget (1{,}000 generations) the
384:control kept descending, overtook the stagnant seeded populations, and the
385:effect reversed sign (from $-34$ to $+67$ on tai30\_20\_04 with full v2
386:seeding). The same check showed the elitist ABC's seeded advantage
387:\emph{persisting} at convergence ($-46$ to $-35$ with GP seeds), and the
388:strong local-search solvers saturating at equal quality. A separate
389:isolation experiment --- attaching the identical tabu local search of
390:TS-N2 to a GA frame --- initially suggested the GA frame benefited from
391:seeds where the ABC frame did not; extending its time cap dissolved that
392:difference too, tracing it to budget starvation rather than to the frame.
393:\emph{Conclusion: no seeding claim about final quality is meaningful
394:without a convergence check of the unseeded control.}
396:\subsection{Artefact 3: what ``faster'' means}
397:We measure convergence speed with two deliberately different metrics:
398:(A)~time to reach within 1\% of the \emph{arm's own} final value, and
399:(B)~time to reach the \emph{unseeded control's} final quality (tolerance
400:$+0.5\%$). Metric~A is self-referential: an arm that settles quickly on a
401:worse plateau scores well, so an apparent acceleration may be nothing more
402:than earlier stagnation. Metric~B removes that degree of freedom by fixing
403:a common target, at the cost of being undefined on instances where the arm
404:never reaches it --- which makes the proportion of instances reaching the
405:target as much a part of the result as the time itself. Reporting both,
406:with that proportion, prevents the most common over-claim in anytime
407:comparisons.
409:\subsection{What survives the artefacts}
410:With all three corrections in place, the pilot's surviving picture is:
411:(i)~seeding improves converged quality for the elitist ABC on all three
412:instances (Wilcoxon, paired); (ii)~for solvers with strong local search
413:the final quality saturates but metric-A acceleration remains; (iii)~for
414:the plain GA the apparent gain is an artefact; (iv)~GP and v2 seeds are
415:statistically tied at the top of the generator ladder, MOR seeds never
416:help; and (v)~initial-population quality ranks exactly as the pool ladder
417:predicts. These five statements were fixed before the final experiment and
418:are what it tests at scale; Section~\ref{sec:res-hyp} reports which of them
419:survived.
