660:Table~\ref{tab:conv} shows that the restriction leaves two of the four
661:solvers unchanged in every respect that matters. fEABCLS keeps the same
662:best arm and the same ordering. ABCE3 keeps its conclusion --- V2 and MIX
663:are separated by a hundredth of a point and both beat the control by more
664:than a point --- while its absolute level rises, because the cells it fails
665:are the small $15\times15$ ones on which it does best.
667:Because the rule is evaluated on the control alone, the sound comparison is
668:the one restricted to instances where \emph{both} the control and the arm
669:being compared plateau. Convergence rates differ enough between arms to
670:make this matter: on fEABCLS the mixed pool plateaus on 44 of 61 instances
671:against the control's 36, while on the GA the randomised
672:Giffler--Thompson pool plateaus on only 8. Table~\ref{tab:inter} reports
673:those paired intersections.
675:The GA is the first exception, and the difference is not cosmetic. Over all
676:61 instances the unseeded control is the best arm (7.52 against MIX's
677:7.58); over the 10 instances where control and mixed pool both plateau, the
678:ordering reverses and the mixed pool wins by 0.69 points (3.45 against
679:4.14). The two other pools that help at convergence, V2H and GP, reverse by
680:a similar margin, while MOR and GT remain harmful there ($+2.10$ and
681:$+1.28$). The negative result for the GA is therefore a statement about
682:\emph{insufficient budget}, not about seeding as such: it is the solver
683:that needs the most time to consolidate a seeded population, and on the
684:cells where it is given that time the harm disappears for the pools that
685:were never the problem. This revises the reading of
686:Section~\ref{sec:whyga}: what the GA lacks is not compatibility with
687:heterogeneous parents in principle, but enough generations to exploit them
688:under these budgets.
690:TSN2 is the second exception, and it cuts the other way. Over all 61
691:instances its mixed pool is the best arm (3.41 against the control's 3.60);
692:over the 35 instances where both plateau, the margin vanishes entirely
693:($2.34$ against $2.32$). The cells TSN2 fails are its largest --- all ten
694:$50\times20$ instances and eight of the ten $30\times20$ --- which are
695:precisely where Section~\ref{sec:res-final} locates its gain. The benefit
696:seeding brings to the strongest solver is thus confined to cells where its
697:budget did not suffice to converge, a materially weaker claim than the
698:unrestricted table supports, and we report it as such.
700:The conclusions are not equally robust to how the rule is set. Varying the
701:threshold over $\{0.05, 0.1, 0.25\}\%$ and the required fraction of runs
702:over $\{0.5, 0.8, 0.9, 1.0\}$, the best arm on the GA is the mixed pool in
703:eleven of the twelve combinations, and on ABCE3 it is always a seeded arm.
704:On TSN2 it is not stable: the mixed pool wins when half the runs must
705:satisfy the rule, and the unseeded control wins when four fifths or nine
706:tenths must. We therefore state the TSN2 result as conditional on that
707:choice rather than as a finding.
709:Unless stated otherwise, the tables that follow use all 61 instances, so
710:that every solver is evaluated on the same instance set; results restricted
711:to converged cells are reported wherever they change a conclusion, which is
712:the GA and TSN2.
714:\subsection{Final quality at the budget}\label{sec:res-final}
716:Table~\ref{tab:rpd} reports mean RPD over the 61 instances. Three regimes
717:are immediately visible, and they do \emph{not} order themselves by solver
718:strength.
720:\begin{table}[t]
721:\centering
722:\caption{Mean RPD (\%) with respect to published lower bounds, over 61
723:instances and 30 runs per cell. Lower is better; the best arm per solver is
724:in bold. A0 is the unseeded control.}
725:\label{tab:rpd}
726:\begin{tabular}{lccccccc}
727:\toprule
728:Solver & A0 & V2H & V2 & MOR & GT & GP & MIX \\
729:\midrule
730:GA      & \textbf{7.52} & 7.66 & 8.05 & 9.70  & 8.47  & 7.99 & 7.58 \\
731:ABCE3   & 10.51 & 9.51 & 9.39 & 10.71 & 10.20 & 9.68 & \textbf{9.37} \\
732:fEABCLS & 3.67  & \textbf{3.66} & \textbf{3.66} & 5.16  & 4.47  & 4.04 & 3.67 \\
733:TSN2    & 3.60  & 3.64 & 3.62 & 4.25  & 3.99  & 3.67 & \textbf{3.41} \\
734:\bottomrule
735:\end{tabular}
736:\end{table}
739:\paragraph{ABCE3: large and uniform gains.} The elitist bee colony is the
740:weakest solver in the portfolio (10.51\% RPD unseeded) and benefits
741:massively. MIX and V2 each win on 35 of 61 instances and V2H on 32, and all
742:three \emph{lose on none}, with $\hat{A}_{12}$ between 0.266 and 0.298.
743:Even GP, a mid-ranked generator elsewhere, wins 30 and loses none. This is
744:the textbook case: the solver's own diversification never reaches the
745:quality already present in the seeds.
747:\paragraph{TSN2: a gain confined to the largest instances.} The
748:tabu-search hybrid is the strongest solver, and the natural expectation ---
749:which our own pilot supported --- is saturation. It does not saturate, but
750:the effect must be stated carefully. MIX wins 15 instances and loses 5
751:($\Delta=-5.5$, $\hat{A}_{12}=0.436$), lowering RPD from 3.60\% to 3.41\%,
752:i.e.\ removing 5.3\% of the excess over the lower bound. It is also the
753:only arm that improves this solver: every other pool is neutral or harmful.
754:In aggregate the gain is small, and it is \emph{not} uniform: MIX is better
755:on 35 instances and worse on 22, with a median relative change of
756:$-0.03\%$. The gain is almost entirely attributable to one size class, and
757:we quantify this in Section~\ref{sec:res-size}. The defensible claim is
758:therefore not that seeding improves the strongest solver in general, but that it does
759:so on large instances under a search budget that is tight relative to
760:instance size.
762:\paragraph{fEABCLS: neutrality.} The memetic ABC is statistically
763:indistinguishable from its control under the three best generators (MIX
764:12/38/11, V2 7/44/10, V2H 7/43/11, all $\hat{A}_{12}\approx0.50$). Its
765:hill-climbing component reaches seed-level quality unaided within the
766:budget. Note that neutrality here is a property of the \emph{good} pools
767:only: MOR wins nothing and loses 56 of 61.
769:\paragraph{GA: harm, but only under an insufficient budget.} Over all 61
770:instances no arm improves the genetic algorithm: the best, MIX, is 13/29/19
771:with $\hat{A}_{12}=0.527$, and V2 is a clear loss (9/25/27). The
772:qualification of Section~\ref{sec:res-conv} is essential here. The GA fails
773:the plateau rule on 49 of 61 instances --- more than any other solver ---
774:and on the 12 where its control does converge the ordering reverses, with
775:MIX at 4.00\% against the control's 4.75\%. The reversal in the pooled
776:table is therefore a statement about the budget this solver needs, not
777:about seeding being harmful to it in principle.
779:Across all four solvers the generator ranking is stable in its extremes:
780:MOR is the worst seeded arm and GT the second worst on every solver (MOR
781:reaches $\hat{A}_{12}=0.920$ on the GA and $0.885$ on fEABCLS), while MIX
782:holds the best Friedman rank among seeded arms on all four
783:(Section~\ref{sec:res-ranking}). On mean RPD, MIX is the best seeded arm on
784:three solvers and is within 0.01 points of the best on fEABCLS. The
785:positions in between are not stable: GP outranks V2 on the GA but not on
786:the other three. The learned policy is thus competitive with the best tuned

