1021:One caution applies to these curves. They record the best individual
1022:\emph{in the population} at each sampling point rather than the solution
1023:the solver finally returns, and the last sample precedes termination by up
1024:to one sampling interval. In practice the two agree closely: the curve
1025:endpoint differs from the final quality of Table~\ref{tab:rpd} by a median
1026:of $0.000\%$ on ABCE3, $0.003\%$ on fEABCLS and $0.029\%$ on the GA. On
1027:TSN2 the curve ends $0.041\%$ \emph{below} the returned quality, because
1028:TS-N2 ranks internally by LEX2 while the curve tracks
1029:$\mathbb{E}[C_{\max}]$, so a schedule that is better on the midpoint need
1030:not be the one it returns. We nonetheless confine every cross-arm
1031:comparison to a single solver, where all arms are measured identically.
1033:Within that scope, the aggregate view exposes a result the final-quality
1034:tables cannot show: \emph{at short budgets seeding is a large win for every
1035:solver in the portfolio, but by margins that differ by an order of
1036:magnitude between them}. At 10\% of budget MIX cuts mean RPD from
1037:$14.84\%$ to $8.91\%$ on fEABCLS and from $12.48\%$ to $7.89\%$ on TSN2,
1038:but only from $12.28\%$ to $10.23\%$ on ABCE3 and from $13.58\%$ to
1039:$12.10\%$ on the GA --- reductions of $40\%$, $37\%$, $17\%$ and $11\%$ in
1040:the excess over the lower bound, equivalent to $5.2\%$, $4.1\%$, $1.8\%$
1041:and $1.3\%$ of makespan. The two largest short-budget gains therefore
1042:accrue to the two \emph{strongest} solvers, which is the reverse of the
1043:ordering at the budget limit. Both are the solvers whose control spends its
1044:opening on an unseeded random population before local search can act:
1045:fEABCLS falls from $14.84\%$ to $5.60\%$ between a tenth and a quarter of
1046:its budget, and seeding buys precisely that opening. The practical reading
1047:is that the question ``does seeding help?'' is ill-posed unless the budget
1048:is specified, and that which solver benefits most depends on where in its
1049:own trajectory the budget cuts.
1051:The GA panel isolates the reversal. Its seeded arms lead until roughly
1052:20\% of the budget, after which the control crosses and stays below to the
1053:end. This is the anytime signature of the consolidation failure analysed in
1054:Section~\ref{sec:whyga}: the seeded population is ahead while quality is
1055:inherited from the seeds, and falls behind as soon as progress must come
1056:from recombination. ABCE3 shows the opposite profile, a gap that opens
1057:immediately and never closes.
1059:The generator ranking is also stable along the whole trajectory, not merely
1060:at the end. MOR is the uppermost seeded curve in all four panels and GT the
1061:next, so their disadvantage is not an artefact of where measurement stops:
1062:from a quarter of the budget onwards both are worse than the control on the
1063:two ABC variants. At a tenth of the budget they are still ahead of it,
1064:which says more about how poor an unseeded random population is at that
1065:point than about any merit of theirs. MIX is the lowest curve in three of
1066:four panels for essentially the entire run. Two observations qualify the
1067:ranking. First, GP is competitive with MIX at very short budgets --- on
1068:TSN2 at 10\% of budget GP reaches $7.94\%$ against MIX's $7.89\%$ --- so
1069:the advantage of the mixed pool \emph{widens as the search proceeds} rather
1070:than being inherited from higher initial seed quality. That is exactly what
1071:one expects if the operative variable is pool diversity rather than seed
1072:quality, and Section~\ref{sec:res-seedqual} confirms it directly. Second,
1073:V2 degrades relative to V2H on the GA as the budget grows ($8.14\%$ versus
1074:$7.73\%$ at convergence), a hint that seeding the entire population with a
1075:single learned policy is worse than seeding half of it, and that some
1076:random material is worth retaining.
1078:Figure~\ref{fig:anytime} restricts the same analysis to the $50\times20$
1079:class in absolute time, where the seeding effect on the strong solvers is
1080:concentrated.
1082:\begin{figure}[t]
1083:\centering
1084:\includegraphics[width=\linewidth]{../figs/anytime_50x20.png}
1085:\caption{Convergence on the $50\times20$ class (10 instances, 30 runs).
1086:Mean RPD with respect to the published lower bound against CPU time, for
1087:the unseeded control and the two best seeded arms. Note the differing time
1088:axes: solver budgets are calibrated per solver and size class.}
1089:\label{fig:anytime}
1090:\end{figure}
1092:Two features are specific to this class. ABCE3's control flattens at
1093:approximately 11.2\% while both seeded arms flatten near 9.7\%, a gap
1094:present from the first seconds that never closes --- a genuine improvement
1095:in converged quality rather than an acceleration. TSN2's control exhibits a
1096:sharp step near 50\,s, when the tabu phase first engages, and thereafter
1097:descends steadily; MIX remains below it over the entire trajectory and is
1098:still below at the budget limit (approximately 3.3\% against 4.1\%). The
1099:separation is therefore not an artefact of where the budget stops.
1101:One methodological consequence deserves emphasis. The GA result would have
1102:been reported as a \emph{success} under any budget shorter than its
1103:crossover point, which is precisely the artefact documented in
1104:Section~\ref{sec:pilot}. Since seeding studies commonly fix a budget
1105:without verifying convergence, this is a plausible source of optimistic
1106:seeding claims in the literature, and it argues for reporting anytime
1107:profiles rather than single-budget summaries.
1109:\subsection{Time to target}\label{sec:res-ttt}
1111:Anytime curves show \emph{that} seeded arms lead early; time-to-target
1112:quantifies \emph{by how much}. We report the two metrics of
1113:Section~\ref{sec:pilot}: (A) the time to come within 1\% of the arm's own
1114:final value, and (B) the time to reach the unseeded control's final quality
1115:within $+0.5\%$. Both are expressed as a fraction of the per-instance
1116:budget so that classes with different budgets can be pooled, and both are
1117:medians over the 61 instances. Metric B is reported together with the
1118:proportion of instances on which the arm reaches the target at all, since a
1119:median computed only over successes is meaningless without it.
