854:Three readings change relative to the per-instance tally.
856:\paragraph{ABCE3 is unaffected by the stricter analysis.} MIX improves the
857:solver by $1.14$ percentage points of RPD with a confidence interval of
858:$[-1.36,-0.93]$ and $p<10^{-3}$ after correction; V2, V2H and GP are
859:likewise unambiguous. Correcting over all 24 contrasts of the study rather
860:than within solvers changes nothing: the same 15 remain significant.
862:\paragraph{The GA is not harmed by the mixed pool.} Its difference is
863:$+0.055$ points with an interval spanning zero and $p=0.55$. What is
864:significantly harmful on this solver is every \emph{single-generator} pool:
865:MOR ($+2.18$), GT ($+0.95$), V2 ($+0.53$) and GP ($+0.47$). Combined with
866:Section~\ref{sec:res-conv}, the picture is that the GA is damaged by
867:homogeneous seeds under an insufficient budget, not by seeding as such.
869:\paragraph{The improvement on TSN2 does not reach significance.} This is
870:the reading that changes most. The signed-rank test over the 61
871:per-instance differences gives $p=0.065$ before correction and $p=0.26$
872:after, so MIX cannot be declared better than the control by a test that
873:weights every instance equally. The bootstrap interval for the mean
874:difference, $[-0.35,-0.045]$, nonetheless excludes zero. The two statistics
875:are not in conflict: the signed-rank test asks about the median difference,
876:which is essentially zero because 51 of the 61 instances are unaffected,
877:while the mean is pulled negative by the ten $50\times20$ instances that
878:carry $94.8\%$ of the effect (Table~\ref{tab:tsn2class}). We therefore
879:state the TSN2 result as what it is --- a small mean improvement
880:concentrated in one size class, with an interval that excludes zero but
881:without significance under an instance-weighted test --- and not as a
882:general improvement of the strongest solver. Establishing the latter would
883:require either more instances in the affected regime or a budget under
884:which more of them behave like $50\times20$.
886:\subsection{The effect scales with instance size}\label{sec:res-size}
888:Aggregate figures conceal a strong interaction with problem size.
889:Figure~\ref{fig:size} plots the relative change in $\mathbb{E}[C_{\max}]$
890:under MIX seeding against instance class, ordered by number of operations
891:(100 to 1000).
893:\begin{figure}[t]
894:\centering
895:\includegraphics[width=\linewidth]{../figs/main_effect_by_size.png}
896:\caption{Relative change in $\mathbb{E}[C_{\max}]$ under MIX seeding with
897:respect to the unseeded control, by instance class (ordered by number of
898:operations). Negative values favour seeding. The benefit for ABCE3 and
899:TSN2 grows with instance size, whereas the penalty for the GA persists.}
900:\label{fig:size}
901:\end{figure}
903:Two trends emerge. For ABCE3 the gain deepens with size, from $-0.21\%$ on
904:the smallest instance to $-1.24\%$ on $20\times20$ and $-1.50\%$ on
905:$50\times20$. For TSN2 the effect is flat and near zero up to $50\times15$
906:and then drops sharply to $-1.06\%$ on $50\times20$ --- and on that class
907:\emph{every} seeded arm beats the control, including GP ($\Delta=-30.9$),
908:MOR ($\Delta=-25.6$) and GT ($\Delta=-19.6$), all three of which are
909:harmful everywhere else. The GA moves in the opposite direction, remaining
910:between $+0.05\%$ and $+0.48\%$ on the four largest classes.
912:Table~\ref{tab:tsn2class} makes the concentration explicit for TSN2, and it
913:is the single most important caveat in this paper. The ten $50\times20$
914:instances account for $94.8\%$ of the aggregate improvement; the remaining
915:51 instances contribute almost nothing. Reporting only the pooled figure of
916:Table~\ref{tab:rpd} would therefore misrepresent the result as a general
917:improvement of the strongest solver, which it is not.
