941:The interpretation is a budget-starvation argument. On $50\times20$
942:instances (1000 operations) even a strong tabu search cannot,
943:within the 900\,s cap, recover from a random start the structure that a
944:constructive heuristic supplies for free. Seeding does not make the search
945:better; it removes a phase of the search that the budget cannot afford.
946:This predicts that the benefit of seeding should be reported as a function
947:of the budget-to-size ratio rather than as a single aggregate figure, and
948:that published seeding gains obtained on small instances will not transfer.
950:\subsection{Anytime behaviour}\label{sec:res-anytime}
952:Final quality alone cannot distinguish a solver that is genuinely improved
953:by seeding from one that is merely given a head start. Because per-solver
954:budgets range from 60\,s to 900\,s across size classes, absolute time is
955:not comparable across instances; we therefore normalise each trace to the
956:fraction of its own budget consumed, the standard convention for aggregated
957:anytime curves, and average over instances on a common grid so that no
958:instance enters or leaves mid-curve. Figure~\ref{fig:anytimeall} shows the
959:resulting profiles over all 61 instances, and Table~\ref{tab:budgetfrac}
960:gives the values at five budget fractions.
