1174:Metric A behaves exactly as the pilot warned, and one figure shows why. On
1175:the GA the earliest plateau belongs to MOR ($0.49$ of the budget against the
1176:control's $0.60$), and MOR is the worst generator on all four solvers: an
1177:arm that settles quickly on a poor value scores well \emph{because} it is
1178:poor. We report the metric to document that it must not be used alone, and
1179:do not tabulate it further.
1181:Metric B needs care of a different kind. An arm that never reaches the
1182:target on an instance has no time-to-target there, and dropping such
1183:instances would summarise only the successes --- precisely discarding the
1184:cases where the arm does worst. The reach rate and the times must therefore
1185:enter the analysis together.
1187:Two properties of the design constrain how. First, the arms are paired:
1188:each seeded arm and its control share the instance, the budget and the
1189:target, so a comparison that treats them as independent groups is not
1190:available. Second, and more restrictive, the target on an instance is the
1191:control's own endpoint, so the control attains it on all 61 instances by
1192:construction. Its sample cannot be censored, whereas the seeded samples
1193:can. A log-rank test against such a reference contrasts a censored sample
1194:with one that is uncensorable by definition, and we therefore do not report
1195:one.
1197:What survives both constraints is a paired analysis of the recorded times,
1198:with non-reachers recorded at the budget, $T=1.0$. That recording
1199:understates a non-reacher's true time, and since only seeded arms are ever
1200:non-reachers, the understatement runs in favour of seeding. It biases the
1201:mean but not the sign: an arm recorded at $1.0$ has $T\ge T_{A0}$ and is
1202:always counted as later. We accordingly take the sign test on the paired
1203:earlier/later counts as the primary contrast, and report the paired
1204:difference in restricted mean time ($\Delta$RMTT, with a bootstrap interval
1205:over instances) as a secondary quantity whose bias is known and runs in one
1206:direction.
1208:Under this treatment ABCE3 stands out. Its mixed pool reaches the control's
1209:\emph{final} quality on every instance and earlier on 57 of the 59 that are
1210:not ties, cutting restricted mean time from $0.38$ to $0.12$ of the budget;
1211:V2 and V2H do nearly as well. These are unambiguous ``same quality,
1212:sooner'' results, and they agree with the converged-quality finding for
1213:that solver. TSN2 joins it more modestly: its mixed pool is earlier on 42
1214:instances and later on 15 ($p=0.003$), and reaches the target on $93\%$ of
1215:them against the control's $100\%$ by construction.
1217:The other two solvers give the opposite answer. \emph{Five of the GA's six
1218:seeded arms are significantly slower} to the control's own final quality;
1219:only the mixed pool escapes, and narrowly (18 earlier, 37 later,
1220:$p=0.057$), though its restricted mean time is clearly worse ($+0.09$,
1221:$[+0.04,+0.13]$). That is the same pattern the final-quality analysis found
1222:on this solver: every single-generator pool harms it and the mixed pool
1223:does not. On fEABCLS the three competitive pools --- V2H, V2 and MIX ---
1224:are statistically indistinguishable from the control ($p=1.00$ for V2 and
1225:MIX), while the three weak ones are significantly slower. Seeding buys that
1226:solver nothing in time.
1228:Mean and sign agree almost everywhere. They part on three arms: MIX on the
1229:GA, V2H on fEABCLS and GT on TSN2, where $\Delta$RMTT reaches significance
1230:and the sign test does not. In each the difference is concentrated in a
1231:minority of instances rather than being a general shift, which is exactly
1232:what the sign test is there to detect.
1234:Ten arm--solver pairs are significantly \emph{slower} to the target than
1235:their control, and MOR is the extreme of every one: it is slower on all
1236:four solvers and reaches the target on no instance at all on the GA and
1237:fEABCLS, where 59 of 61 comparisons go against it. GT is slower on two
1238:solvers and, revealingly, \emph{faster} on ABCE3 --- the solver that
1239:tolerates every pool. Both generators sit at the bottom of the quality
1240:ladder of Section~\ref{sec:res-seedqual}, so this is the one place in the
1241:study where seed quality and outcome move together.
1243:The contrast with a success-only tally is instructive. Computing medians
1244:over successes alone would have credited MOR on the GA with reaching the
1245:target at 71\% of budget, a figure derived from the 15\% of instances where
1246:it succeeds at all.
1248:\subsection{Initial-population quality: the mixed pool does not start
1249:better}\label{sec:res-seedqual}
1251:The obvious explanation for MIX's advantage would be that its seeds are
1252:simply better. They are not. Table~\ref{tab:seedqual} reports the quality
1253:of the initial population each arm actually receives, read from the
1254:step-zero record of the traces, so it is the solvers' own evaluation rather
1255:than the value stored with the pool.
1257:\begin{table}[t]
1258:\centering
1259:\caption{Quality of the seeded initial population relative to the unseeded
1260:control, averaged over five instances spanning $20\times20$ to
1261:$50\times20$ (TSN2 traces). Negative is better. ``Mean'' is over all 250
1262:initial individuals and is the quantity that characterises the pool.}
1263:\label{tab:seedqual}
1264:\begin{tabular}{lcc}
1265:\toprule
1266:Arm & Best individual & Mean of the 250 \\
1267:\midrule
1268:V2H & $-11.34\%$ & $-7.35\%$ \\
1269:V2  & $-11.84\%$ & $-14.73\%$ \\
1270:MOR & $-5.28\%$  & $-10.40\%$ \\
1271:GT  & $-4.75\%$  & $-8.85\%$ \\
1272:GP  & $\mathbf{-12.09\%}$ & $\mathbf{-15.48\%}$ \\
1273:MIX & $-12.25\%$ & $-13.12\%$ \\
1274:\bottomrule
1275:\end{tabular}
1276:\end{table}
1278:On the mean of the initial population --- the natural measure of pool
1279:quality --- MIX is \emph{worse} than both V2 and GP, by 1.6 and 2.4
1280:percentage points respectively, and it never holds the best individual by a
1281:meaningful margin. It nonetheless finishes ahead of both
1282:(Table~\ref{tab:rpd}). A pool that starts worse and ends better is direct
1283:evidence that the operative variable is composition, not seed quality.
1285:This also disposes of a possible confound. The mixed pool is assembled from
1286:exactly the same 1{,}024 solutions per generator that feed the V2, GT and
1287:GP arms --- it introduces no new material, only a different mixture --- so
1288:no difference in provenance or generation effort can be invoked to explain
1289:its advantage.
1291:\subsection{Which seeding strategy? Generator ranking and its
1292:solver-dependence}\label{sec:res-ranking}
1294:The practitioner's question is not whether seeding helps in general but
1295:which pool to use, and whether that choice must be re-made for every
1296:solver. We answer both with a Friedman test over the seven arms with the 61
1297:instances as blocks, followed by Holm-corrected paired post-hoc
1298:comparisons against the top-ranked arm of each solver.
1299:Table~\ref{tab:ranking} reports the mean ranks.
1301:\begin{table}[t]
