1324:Two findings follow, and they answer different questions.
1326:\paragraph{Which generator: the answer varies little across solvers.}
1327:The ranking of the six seeded arms is highly consistent across the
1328:portfolio: restricted to those arms, the mean pairwise Spearman correlation
1329:between the four rank vectors is $\rho=0.88$. MIX is the best
1330:\emph{seeded} arm for all four solvers, with ranks between 2.41 and 2.95,
1331:and is never significantly worse than the top-ranked arm of any solver ---
1332:indeed it is the top-ranked arm outright for three of the four. MOR is last
1333:for all four (5.37 to 6.52) and GT second-to-last for three. With six arms
1334:and four solvers a rank correlation of $0.88$ is a descriptive summary
1335:rather than a demonstration of invariance, and the intermediate positions
1336:do move: GP outranks V2 on the GA but not elsewhere. What is stable is the
1337:top and the bottom, which is what a practitioner needs: within this
1338:portfolio, use a mixed pool and avoid the low-diversity constructive pools,
1339:without re-tuning that choice per algorithm.
1341:\paragraph{Whether to seed at all: the answer does depend on the solver.}
1342:The only arm whose rank moves substantially across solvers is the unseeded
1343:control, from 2.69 on the GA (where it is the top-ranked arm overall) to
1344:5.60 on ABCE3 (where it ranks sixth of seven, beaten by every seeded arm
1345:except MOR). Including A0 drags the mean pairwise agreement down from
1346:$\rho=0.88$ to $\rho=0.69$, and the weakest pair, GA versus ABCE3, rises
1347:from $\rho=0.36$ to $\rho=0.83$ once the control is removed. The
1348:displacement of A0 is thus the entire source of cross-solver disagreement.
1350:This dissociation is the cleanest statement of the paper's result. The
1351:question ``which seeds are good?'' has a solver-independent answer, because
1352:it is a property of the pool. The question ``should I seed?'' does not,
1353:because it depends on whether the solver's variation operators can exploit
1354:the pool --- the mechanism analysed in Section~\ref{sec:whyga}. Reporting
1355:only the first, as seeding studies commonly do, produces a recommendation
1356:that silently fails on solvers like the GA.
1358:\paragraph{Why the advantage is not an artefact of sampling.} A natural
1359:objection is that MIX might win because it exposes the search to more
1360:distinct seed material. Three properties of the design rule this out. The
1361:five pools are the same size ($L=1024$), so every arm draws 30 blocks of
1362:250 under the same arithmetic. The mixed pool is assembled from precisely
1363:the solutions that feed the V2, GT and GP arms, so it contains no material
1364:those arms do not also see. And directly measured
1365:(Section~\ref{sec:res-seedqual}), the population MIX actually receives is
1366:on average \emph{worse} than the ones V2 and GP receive, yet it converges
1367:to better solutions. Sampling breadth and seed quality both point the wrong
1368:way for the objection; within-population generator diversity is what
1369:remains.
