1418:\subsection{Threats addressed by design}\label{sec:res-threats}
1420:Before the checks themselves, one inferential property of the time-to-target
1421:design needs establishing rather than assuming. Its target is
1422:\emph{endogenous}: it is drawn from the control's own endpoint, so the
1423:control is measured against a quantity taken from its own realisation while
1424:the seeded arm is not. That asymmetry is not obviously harmless, and it is
1425:not enough to observe that censoring cannot reverse a sign. We therefore
1426:measured it. Splitting each instance's thirty control runs at random into
1427:two halves of fifteen, averaging each half and treating one as control and
1428:the other as arm, reproduces the analysis under a null that is true by
1429:construction. Over 400 such replicates the sign test rejects at $4.3\%$ on
1430:the GA, $6.8\%$ on ABCE3, $4.0\%$ on fEABCLS and $3.3\%$ on TSN2 against a
1431:nominal $5\%$, so the procedure is close to calibrated. It is not exactly
1432:so: under the null the seeded arm counts as earlier on only $46.2\%$,
1433:$45.3\%$, $47.3\%$ and $48.7\%$ of instances respectively, a small tilt
1434:against seeding. Every sign test in Table~\ref{tab:ttt} is taken against
1435:that solver's own null proportion rather than against $0.5$, which is what
1436:removes the tilt; the effect is visible on the GA, where the mixed pool
1437:reads as significantly slower against $0.5$ and does not against $0.462$.
1439:Two properties of the plateau rule of Section~\ref{sec:res-conv} qualify
1440:what it can be asked to support. Its \emph{counts} are sensitive to how it
1441:is set:
1442:converged cells move from 114 at a $0.05\%$ threshold to 162 at $0.25\%$,
1443:and from 194 to 63 as the fraction of runs required to satisfy it goes from
1444:half to all. Only the conclusions drawn from those counts are reported as
1445:stable, and only where they are. And on TS-N2, 325 of the 1830 control runs
1446:record a \emph{negative} improvement, down to $-1.95\%$: the framework's
1447:statistic takes the best individual under the solver's own ranking, which
1448:for TS-N2 is LEX2, so the recorded incumbent's midpoint need not be
1449:monotone even though its LEX2 value is. Those runs satisfy a rule that asks
1450:for improvement below $0.1\%$ for a reason unrelated to convergence, which
1451:inflates that solver's converged count by an amount we cannot separate
1452:cleanly.
1454:Three further checks guard the numbers above. First, expected makespans were
1455:recomputed by an \emph{independent} verifier: a standalone program that
1456:shares no code with the solver. It re-implements the instance parser, the
1457:interval arithmetic and the decoding from scratch, depends only on the
1458:standard library, and includes no header of the framework, so a defect in
1459:the solver's scheduling or interval classes cannot hide behind it. The
1460:sample is systematic rather than random --- every combination of the four
1461:solvers and seven arms on one instance from each of the seven size classes,
1462:980 stored solutions in total --- so each solver, arm and class is
1463:represented.
1465:The verifier reproduces the reported interval makespan exactly in 979 of
1466:the 980 cases. This is the expected outcome once one accounts for
1467:Lamarckian replacement: after local search the framework re-encodes the
1468:improved schedule into the chromosome, so the stored sequence already
1469:carries the machine orders of the schedule that was actually executed, and
1470:decoding it returns that same schedule. The single exception, one run on
1471:\texttt{tai50\_20\_09}, differs by 0.5 units of $\mathbb{E}[C_{\max}]$ on a
1472:value of 3119 --- $0.016\%$, and in the direction that makes the reported
1473:figure the more pessimistic. We have not traced its cause and record it as
1474:an open point. Second, every arm of a solver runs on the same instance
1475:with
1476:the same seed indices and the same budget, so the design is blocked by
1477:instance and no arm is systematically advantaged; the qualification of
1478:Section~\ref{sec:design} on what that pairing does and does not guarantee
1479:applies. Third, the stopping rule is a fixed CPU-time budget per size
1480:class, identical across arms, which eliminates the stagnation-triggered
1481:artefact documented in Section~\ref{sec:pilot}.
1483:\section{Discussion}\label{sec:discussion}
1485:\subsection{Seeding quality is not the operative variable}
1487:The pre-specified hypothesis --- that seeding helps precisely those
1488:solvers whose own search saturates below the seed level --- is not
1489:supported. Ordering the portfolio by unseeded RPD gives ABCE3 (10.51) $>$
1490:GA (7.52) $>$ fEABCLS (3.67) $>$ TSN2 (3.60), but the seeding effect
1491:follows the order ABCE3 (large gain) $>$ TSN2 (small gain) $>$ fEABCLS
1492:(null) $>$ GA (loss at the budget, gain once the budget suffices). The
1493:strongest solver in the portfolio benefits while a mid-ranked one appears
1494:harmed, so solver quality alone does not predict the sign of the effect.
1496:Two variables do carry information, and the design supports them as
1497:associations rather than isolating them as causes. The first is the
1498:\emph{budget relative to what the solver needs}. Section~\ref{sec:res-conv}
1499:shows the GA reversal to be a budget effect, and Section~\ref{sec:res-size}
1500:shows the TSN2 gain confined to the class where the budget is tightest
1501:relative to instance size. The two findings point the same way: seeding
1502:matters most where the solver has least time to build structure itself, and
1503:its apparent sign can flip when that time is short enough.
1505:The second is pool composition. MIX --- equal thirds of v2, GT and GP,
1506:i.e.\ 84/83/83 of the 250 seeded individuals --- holds the best Friedman
1507:rank among seeded arms on all four solvers, while the single-generator
1508:pools of comparable individual quality (V2, GP) are weaker and the
1509:low-diversity pools (MOR, GT) are actively harmful. Since MIX is assembled
1510:from exactly the solutions that feed V2, GT and GP, and starts from a worse
1511:average population than either V2 or GP
1512:(Section~\ref{sec:res-seedqual}), neither provenance nor seed quality can
1513:account for its advantage. What we cannot do is separate diversity from
1514:the other properties a mixture has --- coverage of distinct basins,
1515:complementarity between specific pairs of generators --- because we did not
1516:run pools matched on quality with diversity varied independently. The
1517:composition claim is therefore an association supported by an elimination
1518:of the obvious alternatives, not a controlled result.
1520:\subsection{Why the genetic algorithm is harmed: a failure to
1521:consolidate}\label{sec:whyga}
1523:The intuitive explanation for a seeding-induced loss is premature
1524:convergence: a population of good, similar individuals collapses onto a
1525:local optimum. The data do not support it. We instrumented the GA and
1526:ABCE3 with the mean pairwise Hamming distance of the population, sampled
1527:every 10\,s over 10 runs on four medium instances (normalised to $[0,1]$,
1528:where 0 denotes an identical population). Table~\ref{tab:hamming} reports
1529:the result. The seeded GA is not less diverse than its control --- it is
1530:\emph{significantly more} diverse, by $+24.3\%$ under V2 and $+13.7\%$
1531:under MIX (paired Wilcoxon, $p<10^{-4}$), and it ends the run at $0.720$
1532:against the control's $0.464$.
1534:\begin{table}[t]
1535:\centering
1536:\caption{Mean pairwise Hamming distance of the population (0 = identical
1537:population, 1 = maximal positional difference), averaged over four
1538:instances and 10 runs. The seeded GA converges structurally \emph{less}
1539:than its control; ABCE3 is unaffected by seeding.}
1540:\label{tab:hamming}
1541:\begin{tabular}{llcccc}
1542:\toprule
1543:Solver & Arm & Start & Mean & End & Drop \\
1544:\midrule
1545:\multirow{3}{*}{GA}
1546: & A0  & 0.9474 & 0.7646 & 0.4639 & $-51.0\%$ \\
1547: & V2  & 0.9310 & 0.8845 & 0.7195 & $-22.7\%$ \\
1548: & MIX & 0.9301 & 0.8350 & 0.5552 & $-40.3\%$ \\
1549:\midrule
1550:\multirow{3}{*}{ABCE3}
1551: & A0  & 0.9474 & 0.7140 & 0.8541 & $-9.9\%$ \\
1552: & V2  & 0.9310 & 0.7059 & 0.8602 & $-7.6\%$ \\
1553: & MIX & 0.9301 & 0.7171 & 0.8497 & $-8.6\%$ \\
