1570:The contrast with ABCE3 is consistent with this reading. Seeding does not
1571:perturb ABCE3's diversity trajectory at all (differences of $-2.3\%$ and
1572:$+4.0\%$, $p=0.27$ and $p=0.90$), and its population stays dispersed
1573:throughout ($0.85$ at the end). ABCE3 does not rely on recombining
1574:population members to build structure, so seeds are retained and refined
1575:rather than destroyed.
1577:The ordering of the two seeded arms is itself informative. V2, drawn from a
1578:single generator, produces the least consolidation ($-22.7\%$) and the
1579:largest loss in final quality; MIX, the more heterogeneous pool,
1580:consolidates further ($-40.3\%$) and is the better of the two arms on this
1581:solver. Structural heterogeneity of the \emph{pool} and of the
1582:\emph{surviving population} are therefore not the same quantity: what slows
1583:the GA is a population its operator cannot consolidate, not a diverse pool
1584:as such.
1586:Three limits on this account should be stated plainly. First, it is
1587:consistent with the convergence analysis rather than independent of it:
1588:Section~\ref{sec:res-conv} shows the GA's loss disappears where its control
1589:converges, which is what a consolidation-rate explanation predicts, but
1590:both observations rest on the same runs. Second, GA and ABCE3 differ in
1591:selection, replacement, mutation and population dynamics as well as in
1592:recombination, so attributing the contrast to JOX specifically is
1593:under-determined; establishing it would require holding the rest of the GA
1594:fixed and varying the crossover, which we did not do. Third, mean pairwise
1595:Hamming distance is a genotypic measure on a representation with repeated
1596:symbols, and need not track the building blocks JOX actually manipulates;
1597:a measure of parent--offspring precedence preservation would test the
1598:mechanism more directly. We therefore offer consolidation failure as the
1599:reading the present data favour, not as a demonstrated cause.
1601:\subsection{Practitioner guidance}
1603:The results support three recommendations, each with its scope attached.
1604:(i) Seed with a \emph{mixed} pool: it outranks every single-generator pool
1605:on all four solvers. We did not measure generation cost, so we make no
1606:claim that combining three generators is free; a practitioner working at
1607:very short budgets, where seeding matters most, should measure it. (ii)
1608:Expect the benefit to grow as the budget tightens relative to instance
1609:size, and expect its \emph{sign} to be unreliable when the budget is far
1610:from sufficient --- that is where our genetic algorithm reverses. (iii)
1611:Before concluding that seeding fails on a recombination-based solver,
1612:check whether its control has converged; on our data that distinction
1613:separates a genuine null from an artefact of the budget.
1615:\subsection{Limitations}\label{sec:limits}
1617:\paragraph{Scope.} The study covers one problem family (interval JSP), one
1618:encoding (permutation with repetition), and four solvers three of which
1619:share the ABC framework or components of it. Nothing here licenses a
1620:general statement about recombinative metaheuristics or about seeding at
1621:large; the conclusions are about this problem, this representation and
1622:these operators. Replication on the deterministic JSP, on a different
1623:uncertainty model, or on an external benchmark would be the natural next
1624:test.
1626:\paragraph{Mechanism.} The two explanatory claims --- pool composition
1627:rather than seed quality, and consolidation failure rather than premature
1628:convergence --- are associations that survive the obvious alternatives, not
1629:controlled results. Establishing them would require pools matched on
1630:quality with diversity varied independently, and a GA in which only the
1631:crossover changes. Neither experiment is in this design.
1633:\paragraph{Budgets and cost.} Budgets are CPU-time caps measured with the
1634:process clock on a single Linux machine at a fixed occupancy of fourteen
1635:concurrent workers. Within a cell every arm shares the cap, so the paired
1636:comparisons that carry every claim here are unaffected, but the absolute
1637:level is not portable: the work a CPU-second buys depends on contention for
1638:cache and memory bandwidth among the concurrent workers. Our absolute RPDs
1639:should not be compared against figures published for these solvers
1640:elsewhere, and more generally a single absolute quality figure reported
1641:under a time budget, without the hardware occupancy that produced it, is
1642:less reproducible than it appears. We also do not measure the cost of
1643:\emph{producing} the pools:
1644:the training of the learned policy is excluded as amortised across
1645:instances, and the per-instance generation cost of the four generators was
1646:not instrumented. At the budgets where seeding matters most --- a tenth of
1647:ours --- that omission is material, and we flag it rather than assume it
1648:away.
1650:\paragraph{Design gaps.} The seeded fraction $p^{*}=100\%$ was selected on
1651:two solvers and eight instances, so it is a protocol decision for this
1652:study rather than a generally preferable fraction; the GA's V2H arm, which
1653:is less harmful than V2, suggests the fraction interacts with the solver.
1654:The mixed pool was not compared against two-generator mixtures or against
1655:alternative proportions, so we cannot attribute its advantage to any
1656:particular pairing. And the structural-diversity instrumentation covers
1657:four instances and two solvers, a narrow base for the argument it supports.
1659:\section{Conclusions}\label{sec:conclusions}
1661:We conducted, to our knowledge, the largest controlled study of population
1662:seeding for the interval job shop: four solvers, seven initialisation arms,
1663:61 benchmark instances and 30 runs per cell --- 51{,}240 runs --- evaluated
1664:against published lower bounds under a fixed-budget protocol designed to
1665:eliminate three artefacts we document in a pilot study.
1667:Seeding is not uniformly beneficial, and its sign is not predicted by
1668:solver strength. Analysed with the instance as the unit, a mixed pool
1669:improves the weakest solver decisively ($-1.14$ points of RPD, 95\% CI
1670:$[-1.36,-0.93]$), leaves a memetic ABC unchanged, and does not harm a
1671:genetic algorithm, though every single-generator pool does. On the
1672:strongest solver the mean improvement is small, concentrated in one size
1673:class, short of significance under an instance-weighted test, and absent
1674:altogether on the instances where that solver's own control converges; we
1675:report it as a bounded result rather than as an improvement of the state
1676:of the art.
1678:Three findings seem to us to travel beyond this problem family. First,
1679:\emph{the budget decides the sign, not just the size}. The two effects at
1680:the ends of our portfolio both move when the analysis is restricted to
1681:cells whose control actually converged, and they move in opposite
1682:directions: the genetic algorithm's apparent damage disappears, and the
1683:tabu hybrid's gain disappears with it. Its benefit is confined to the
1684:largest instances, which are exactly the cells where its budget did not
1685:suffice to converge. Neither reversal is visible in a table that pools
1686:converged and budget-limited cells. A seeding study that fixes one budget
1687:without checking convergence can therefore report either sign, and which
1688:one it reports is not predictable from the solver's strength.
1690:Second, \emph{composition beats quality}. The mixed pool is assembled from
1691:exactly the solutions that feed the single-generator arms and receives a
1692:worse average initial population than two of them, yet it holds the best
1693:Friedman rank among seeded arms on all four solvers. Seed quality is not
1694:irrelevant at the bottom of the ladder: MOR, the weakest generator, is
1695:significantly slower to the control's own final quality on all four
1696:solvers, and GT on three of them. But among the pools that help, quality
1697:does not order them, and composition does.
1699:Third, \emph{the failure mode is not the one usually assumed}. Premature
1700:convergence is the standard explanation for seeding going wrong; our
1701:diversity measurements show the opposite, a population that stays dispersed
1702:and never consolidates within the budget. We advance consolidation failure
1703:as the reading the data favour, while noting that separating it from the
1704:other differences between the two solvers would need ablations this design
1705:does not contain.
1707:The practical consequence is that seeding should be treated as an
1708:interaction between pool composition, variation operator and budget,
1709:evaluated at the instance size of the intended application, rather than as
1710:a property of the seeds alone. And the methodological consequence is that
1711:the three habits our pilot identified --- stagnation-triggered stopping,
1712:single-budget comparison, and self-referential speed metrics --- are enough
1713:to manufacture a positive seeding result where none exists, or to hide one
1714:that is there.
1716:%% =========================================================================
